COMMAND NAME: gpstop.sh

Stops or restarts a Greenplum Database system. Can also be used to
reload the postgresql.conf file without a restart.

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpstop.sh [-d master_data_directory] [-B parallel_processes] 
[-z] [-f | -i | -s] [-r] [-m] [-y] [-u] [-a] [-q] [-l 
logfile_directory] [-D] [-t | -? | -v]


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpstop.sh script is analogous to functionality offered by the 
PostgreSQL pg_ctl and postmaster commands, which are used to stop the 
database server. When you stop a Greenplum Database system, you are 
actually stopping several postmaster database server processes at once 
(the master and all of the segment instances). The gpstop.sh script 
handles the shutdown of the individual instances. Each instance is 
shutdown in parallel.

Before you can stop a Greenplum Database system, you must have started 
it using gpstart.sh first.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-d <master_data_directory>

Optional. The master host data directory. If not specified, the value set 
for $MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY will be used.


-B <parallel_processes>

The number of segments to stop in parallel. If not specified, the script 
will start up to 60 parallel processes depending on how many segment 
instances it needs to stop.


-z (force shutdown)

Optional. Forces the shutdown of a segment instance with an invalid status. 
This may be desirable if the segment instance or host had a minor error 
during startup, and you want to shut down the system to fix the error, 
then retry startup again. The default behavior is to not shutdown segment 
instances with an invalid status.


-f (fast shutdown - rollback)

Fast shut down. Any transactions in progress are interrupted and rolled back.


-i (immediate shutdown - abort)

Immediate shut down. Any transactions in progress are aborted. Use this 
option with care if you are running in continue fault tolerance mode 
(read-write), as this could cause data corruption.


-s (smart shutdown - wait for commits)

Smart shut down. Waits for active transactions to complete before shutting 
down. This is the default shutdown mode.


-r (restart)

Restart after shutdown is complete.


-m (master only)

Optional. Shuts down a Greenplum master instance that was started in 
maintenance mode.


-y (do not stop standby master)

Do not stop the standby master process. The default is to stop the standby 
master.


-u (reload postgresql.conf only)

This option tells the master instance to reload the postgresql.conf file but 
does not shutdown the Greenplum Database array. Use this option to make new 
configuration settings active after editing the postgresql.conf file.


-a (do not prompt)

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.


-q (no screen output)

Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on the screen, but is 
still written to the log file.


-l <logfile_directory>

The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-D (debug)

Sets logging level to debug.


-t (show default script settings)

Displays the settings that will be used to shut down the system, such as 
the master data directory and port, and the name, data directory, port, 
valid status, and primary or mirror status of all the segment instances.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


-v (show script version)

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Stop a Greenplum Database system in smart mode:

gpstop.sh


Stop a Greenplum Database system in fast mode:

gpstop.sh -f


Force shutdown of the system if it has failed segment instances from a 
previous startup attempt:

gpstop.sh -z


Stop all segment instances and then restart the system:

gpstop.sh -r


Stop a master instance that was started in maintenance mode:

gpstop.sh -m


Display the online help for the gpstop.sh script:

gpstop.sh -?


Reload the postgresql.conf file after making configuration changes but do 
not shutdown the Greenplum Database array:

gpstop.sh -u


